1
00:00:00,200 --> 00:00:04,654
Right now, the Sun is pulling on you, on the oceans, on the whole planet.

2
00:00:04,654 --> 00:00:06,850
It is lighting up half of the Earth.

3
00:00:06,850 --> 00:00:10,267
But what if, for exactly one second, it simply vanished?

4
00:00:10,267 --> 00:00:11,000
Not forever.

5
00:00:11,000 --> 00:00:11,976
Just one second.

6
00:00:11,976 --> 00:00:14,600
Then it comes back, as if nothing happened.

7
00:00:15,299 --> 00:00:17,655
Most people imagine instant darkness.

8
00:00:17,655 --> 00:00:22,239
The sky goes black, the Earth flies off into space, and everyone panics.

9
00:00:22,239 --> 00:00:24,403
But that is not what would happen.

10
00:00:24,403 --> 00:00:31,470
What actually happens is much stranger, and it reveals just how huge, and how slow, our solar system really is.

11
00:00:32,170 --> 00:00:33,934
So let's run the experiment.

12
00:00:33,934 --> 00:00:37,525
Second by second, minute by minute, and planet by planet.

13
00:00:38,225 --> 00:00:39,422
The Sun disappears.

14
00:00:39,422 --> 00:00:41,313
And on Earth, nothing happens.

15
00:00:41,313 --> 00:00:42,258
Nothing at all.

16
00:00:42,258 --> 00:00:43,581
The sky stays bright.

17
00:00:43,581 --> 00:00:46,354
The Sun is still up there, shining as usual.

18
00:00:46,354 --> 00:00:48,623
Because the sunlight you see is old.

19
00:00:48,623 --> 00:00:53,286
It left the surface of the Sun about eight minutes and twenty seconds ago.

20
00:00:53,986 --> 00:00:56,583
Light is the fastest thing in the universe.

21
00:00:56,583 --> 00:00:59,844
Almost three hundred thousand kilometers every second.

22
00:00:59,844 --> 00:01:04,011
Fast enough to circle the Earth seven and a half times in one second.

23
00:01:04,011 --> 00:01:10,413
And still, the distance to the Sun is so enormous that light needs about five hundred seconds to cross it.

24
00:01:11,113 --> 00:01:17,055
That means, for eight minutes and twenty seconds, the Sun is gone, and nobody on Earth can know it.

25
00:01:17,055 --> 00:01:23,358
Every photo, every sunrise, every warm beach is still running on light that left before the Sun vanished.

26
00:01:24,058 --> 00:01:25,449
But what about gravity?

27
00:01:25,449 --> 00:01:28,110
The Sun is the thing holding Earth in orbit.

28
00:01:28,110 --> 00:01:31,738
If the Sun disappears, shouldn't Earth fly away immediately?

29
00:01:32,438 --> 00:01:34,231
Here is the surprising answer.

30
00:01:34,231 --> 00:01:36,023
Gravity is not instant either.

31
00:01:36,023 --> 00:01:38,892
Changes in gravity travel at the speed of light.

32
00:01:38,892 --> 00:01:47,735
In twenty seventeen, astronomers detected gravitational waves from two colliding neutron stars, about a hundred and thirty million light years away.

33
00:01:47,735 --> 00:01:52,396
A flash of gamma rays from the same event arrived less than two seconds later.

34
00:01:52,396 --> 00:01:57,953
After a journey of a hundred and thirty million years, gravity and light were basically tied.

35
00:01:58,653 --> 00:02:03,567
So Earth keeps orbiting an empty point in space for eight minutes and twenty seconds.

36
00:02:03,567 --> 00:02:06,226
It is following a Sun that is no longer there.

37
00:02:06,926 --> 00:02:11,696
Then, at eight minutes and twenty seconds, the missing second finally arrives.

38
00:02:11,696 --> 00:02:12,062
A gap.

39
00:02:12,062 --> 00:02:16,526
A one second hole in the sunlight, traveling through space like a shadow.

40
00:02:17,226 --> 00:02:20,122
On the day side of Earth, the light switches off.

41
00:02:20,122 --> 00:02:28,219
The blue sky turns black, because the blue is just sunlight bouncing around in the air, and that light is gone in a fraction of a second.

42
00:02:28,219 --> 00:02:30,346
For one second, it is night at noon.

43
00:02:31,046 --> 00:02:32,490
Would you see the stars?

44
00:02:32,490 --> 00:02:33,272
Probably not.

45
00:02:33,272 --> 00:02:36,280
Your eyes need many minutes to adjust to the dark.

46
00:02:36,280 --> 00:02:41,514
You would just see a black flash, like someone flicked the switch of the entire planet.

47
00:02:41,514 --> 00:02:43,078
And then, the Sun is back.

48
00:02:43,778 --> 00:02:47,649
Solar panels around the world drop to zero for that second.

49
00:02:47,649 --> 00:02:52,503
Every solar farm, every satellite panel, every calculator on a windowsill.

50
00:02:53,203 --> 00:02:57,195
And here is a number that shows how much energy the Sun gives us.

51
00:02:57,195 --> 00:03:05,302
In a single second, the sunlight hitting Earth carries roughly as much energy as all of humanity uses in about two and a half hours.

52
00:03:05,302 --> 00:03:09,171
All the cars, factories, power plants and phones on the planet.

53
00:03:09,171 --> 00:03:11,934
That is what we lose in that one dark second.

54
00:03:12,634 --> 00:03:15,617
At the very same moment, the gravity gap arrives.

55
00:03:15,617 --> 00:03:18,843
For one second, Earth is not being pulled by the Sun.

56
00:03:18,843 --> 00:03:22,982
So, for one second, it stops curving and travels in a straight line.

57
00:03:22,982 --> 00:03:28,826
Earth moves about thirty kilometers every second, so it shoots thirty kilometers straight ahead.

58
00:03:29,526 --> 00:03:32,092
How far does that knock us off our orbit?

59
00:03:32,092 --> 00:03:33,594
About three millimeters.

60
00:03:33,594 --> 00:03:38,351
Three millimeters, out of an orbit that is almost a billion kilometers long.

61
00:03:38,351 --> 00:03:41,543
Then gravity returns, and Earth simply keeps going.

62
00:03:41,543 --> 00:03:43,608
No ice age, no flying into space.

63
00:03:43,608 --> 00:03:48,427
Just a three millimeter detour that no instrument on Earth would ever notice.

64
00:03:49,127 --> 00:03:50,060
The temperature?

65
00:03:50,060 --> 00:03:51,343
Practically no change.

66
00:03:51,343 --> 00:03:58,279
The oceans and the air store so much heat that one second without sunlight is like opening the fridge door for a blink.

67
00:03:58,979 --> 00:04:00,956
But the story doesn't end on Earth.

68
00:04:00,956 --> 00:04:07,619
That dark second keeps traveling outward, through the whole solar system, and each planet gets it at a different time.

69
00:04:08,319 --> 00:04:11,141
Mercury goes dark after about three minutes.

70
00:04:11,141 --> 00:04:12,360
Venus, six minutes.

71
00:04:12,360 --> 00:04:15,439
Then Earth, at eight minutes and twenty seconds.

72
00:04:15,439 --> 00:04:17,298
Mars, about thirteen minutes.

73
00:04:17,298 --> 00:04:19,864
Jupiter has to wait forty three minutes.

74
00:04:19,864 --> 00:04:22,558
Saturn, almost an hour and twenty minutes.

75
00:04:22,558 --> 00:04:24,866
Uranus, two hours and forty minutes.

76
00:04:24,866 --> 00:04:28,522
And Neptune, more than four hours after the Sun vanished.

77
00:04:29,222 --> 00:04:30,257
Think about that.

78
00:04:30,257 --> 00:04:37,926
For four hours, someone on Neptune would see a perfectly normal Sun, a Sun that hasn't existed for one second, four hours ago.

79
00:04:38,626 --> 00:04:40,224
And it goes even farther.

80
00:04:40,224 --> 00:04:46,105
Voyager one, launched in nineteen seventy seven, is the most distant human made object ever.

81
00:04:46,105 --> 00:04:53,073
This month, in November twenty twenty six, it becomes the first spacecraft to reach one light day from Earth.

82
00:04:53,073 --> 00:04:56,333
A radio signal takes twenty four hours to reach it.

83
00:04:57,033 --> 00:05:01,343
So the dark second would reach Voyager almost a full day after it happened.

84
00:05:01,343 --> 00:05:05,481
Nearly fifty years of travel, and we have only crossed one day of light.

85
00:05:06,181 --> 00:05:10,707
The nearest star, Proxima Centauri, is about four point two light years away.

86
00:05:10,707 --> 00:05:16,115
If someone there were watching our Sun, they would see the blink more than four years later.

87
00:05:16,115 --> 00:05:19,642
In the Andromeda galaxy, two and a half million years later.

88
00:05:20,342 --> 00:05:22,198
Which also means something else.

89
00:05:22,198 --> 00:05:26,374
When you look at the stars tonight, you are not seeing them as they are.

90
00:05:26,374 --> 00:05:31,304
You are seeing them as they were, years, centuries, sometimes thousands of years ago.

91
00:05:31,304 --> 00:05:33,740
Some of those stars might already be gone.

92
00:05:34,440 --> 00:05:37,676
So, what happens if the Sun disappears for one second?

93
00:05:37,676 --> 00:05:39,295
For eight minutes, nothing.

94
00:05:39,295 --> 00:05:46,728
Then one second of night at noon, a three millimeter detour, and a dark flash racing out to the stars for millions of years.

95
00:05:47,428 --> 00:05:49,123
The universe isn't just big.

96
00:05:49,123 --> 00:05:49,728
It's slow.

97
00:05:49,728 --> 00:05:53,785
Even light, the fastest thing there is, needs time to get anywhere.

98
00:05:54,485 --> 00:05:58,279
Next time, we will go further: what if the Sun disappeared forever?

99
00:05:58,279 --> 00:06:01,167
If you want to see that, subscribe to Cosmic Scale.

100
00:06:01,167 --> 00:06:02,186
See you out there.
